  "page1": {
    "title": "One Singer, a Whole Story",
    "subtitle": "How a single voice and one drum can carry a long Korean tale.",
    "body": "In old Korea, one singer could tell a whole long story. We call this art pansori. The singer stands on a mat and sings for hours. Next to the singer sits a drummer with one drum. This drummer is called the gosu. The gosu keeps the beat and shouts cheers like \"Good!\" These cheers are called <mark>chuimsae</mark>, and they wake the whole crowd. The singer uses one voice for the king and another for the servant. With only a fan, the singer paints every scene. When the story turns sad, the song grows slow and heavy. When it turns happy, the beat runs fast. People sit close and answer back. Because the crowd joins in, each show feels new. Pansori is one voice and one drum.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/venus/2026-07/15.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "A pansori singer with a fan stands on a mat while the gosu drums and the crowd cheers.",
    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "pansori",
        "ko": "판소리 (소리꾼이 이야기를 노래하는 우리 전통 음악)"
